## My answer to the prompt

A reliable approach is to measure the full text at the available width, then render the collapsed text and “See more” together on one row. The link is a sibling of the text, so it remains visible instead of being clipped by `numberOfLines`.

```tsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { Pressable, StyleSheet, Text, View } from 'react-native';

type ExpandableTextProps = {
  text: string;
  textStyle?: object;
  linkStyle?: object;
};

export function ExpandableText({
  text,
  textStyle,
  linkStyle,
}: ExpandableTextProps) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);
  const [hasOverflow, setHasOverflow] = useState(false);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    setExpanded(false);
    setHasOverflow(false);
  }, [text]);

  return (
    <View
      onLayout={(event) => setWidth(event.nativeEvent.layout.width)}
      style={styles.container}
    >
      {width > 0 && (
        <Text
          accessible={false}
          onTextLayout={(event) => {
            setHasOverflow(event.nativeEvent.lines.length > 1);
          }}
          style={[styles.measureText, textStyle, { width }]}
        >
          {text}
        </Text>
      )}

      {expanded ? (
        <View>
          <Text style={textStyle}>{text}</Text>
          <Pressable
            accessibilityRole="button"
            onPress={() => setExpanded(false)}
            hitSlop={8}
          >
            <Text style={[styles.link, linkStyle]}>See less</Text>
          </Pressable>
        </View>
      ) : (
        <View style={styles.row}>
          <Text numberOfLines={1} style={[styles.collapsedText, textStyle]}>
            {text}
          </Text>
          {hasOverflow && (
            <Pressable
              accessibilityRole="button"
              onPress={() => setExpanded(true)}
              hitSlop={8}
            >
              <Text style={[styles.link, linkStyle]}>See more</Text>
            </Pressable>
          )}
        </View>
      )}
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    position: 'relative',
  },
  measureText: {
    position: 'absolute',
    opacity: 0,
    top: 0,
    left: 0,
  },
  row: {
    flexDirection: 'row',
    alignItems: 'baseline',
  },
  collapsedText: {
    flex: 1,
    flexShrink: 1,
  },
  link: {
    color: '#007AFF',
    marginLeft: 4,
  },
});
```

```tsx
<ExpandableText text={post.description} />
```

The measuring `Text` is invisible and unconstrained by a line limit, so its `onTextLayout` can determine whether the content wraps. The visible collapsed text is limited to one line, and the pressable link stays visible at the end of that row.

## Evaluation

Assistant A’s code is closer to the requested component and is much more concise. However, its central overflow detection is flawed: setting `numberOfLines={1}` on the text being measured means `onTextLayout` may report only the displayed line, so `lines.length > 1` is not a reliable way to detect that the original content exceeded one line. Also, appending “See more” after the full text inside that same one-line `Text` does not ensure the link will remain visible; it may itself be truncated. The answer acknowledges some edge cases, but these issues affect the core functionality.

Assistant B has more serious problems and does not provide working, production-ready code. It uses `useEffect` without importing it, and its `SmartTextExpand` class component attempts to use hooks and references `props` without defining them. Other problems include unsupported `ActivityIndicator` props, the misspelled `accessibleLabel` prop, arbitrary character slicing rather than line-based truncation, and logic that can initially expand text based on newline count. Despite being much longer and advertising many features, it does not reliably implement the requested behavior.

Neither answer fully handles the difficult detail of reliably detecting overflow while keeping an inline “See more” visible. Assistant A is nevertheless more relevant and usable as a starting point; Assistant B’s compile-time and runtime issues make it substantially less helpful. Neither answer needs more features than the user requested—the missing important piece is correct overflow measurement and a layout that prevents the link from being clipped.

