## My answer to the prompt

A reliable inline “See more” needs to measure the text at its actual rendered width. `numberOfLines={1}` alone can hide the overflow, so it cannot reliably tell you whether to show the link. The component below measures the full text, then trims its first line until the inline link fits.

```tsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import {
  LayoutChangeEvent,
  StyleProp,
  StyleSheet,
  Text,
  TextStyle,
  View,
} from 'react-native';

type ExpandableTextProps = {
  text: string;
  textStyle?: StyleProp<TextStyle>;
  linkStyle?: StyleProp<TextStyle>;
  moreLabel?: string;
};

export function ExpandableText({
  text,
  textStyle,
  linkStyle,
  moreLabel = 'See more',
}: ExpandableTextProps) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(false);
  const [prefix, setPrefix] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [prefixFits, setPrefixFits] = useState(false);

  const onContainerLayout = (event: LayoutChangeEvent) => {
    const nextWidth = event.nativeEvent.layout.width;
    if (nextWidth !== width) {
      setWidth(nextWidth);
      setExpanded(false);
      setHasMore(false);
      setPrefix(null);
      setPrefixFits(false);
    }
  };

  const onFullTextLayout = (event: any) => {
    const lines = event.nativeEvent.lines as Array<{ text: string }>;
    const overflows = lines.length > 1;
    setHasMore(overflows);
    setPrefix(overflows ? lines[0].text : null);
    setPrefixFits(!overflows);
  };

  const onCandidateLayout = (event: any) => {
    const lines = event.nativeEvent.lines as unknown[];
    if (lines.length > 1 && prefix) {
      // Remove one Unicode code point and check again.
      setPrefix(Array.from(prefix).slice(0, -1).join(''));
    } else {
      setPrefixFits(true);
    }
  };

  const measureStyle = [styles.text, textStyle, { width }];

  return (
    <View style={styles.container} onLayout={onContainerLayout}>
      {width > 0 && (
        <>
          <Text
            style={[measureStyle, styles.hidden]}
            onTextLayout={onFullTextLayout}
            accessible={false}
          >
            {text}
          </Text>

          {hasMore && prefix !== null && !prefixFits && (
            <Text
              style={[measureStyle, styles.hidden]}
              onTextLayout={onCandidateLayout}
              accessible={false}
            >
              {prefix}
              {'… '}
              <Text style={linkStyle}>{moreLabel}</Text>
            </Text>
          )}
        </>
      )}

      {expanded ? (
        <Text style={[styles.text, textStyle]}>{text}</Text>
      ) : hasMore && prefixFits && prefix !== null ? (
        <Text style={[styles.text, textStyle]}>
          {prefix}
          {'… '}
          <Text
            style={linkStyle}
            onPress={() => setExpanded(true)}
            accessibilityRole="button"
          >
            {moreLabel}
          </Text>
        </Text>
      ) : (
        <Text style={[styles.text, textStyle]} numberOfLines={1}>
          {text}
        </Text>
      )}
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    width: '100%',
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 16,
    color: '#333',
  },
  hidden: {
    position: 'absolute',
    opacity: 0,
    top: 0,
    left: 0,
  },
});
```

Usage:

```tsx
<ExpandableText
  text={post.description}
  textStyle={{ fontSize: 16, lineHeight: 22 }}
  linkStyle={{ color: '#007AFF', fontWeight: '600' }}
/>
```

## Evaluation

**Assistant B is better, though neither answer is production-ready as written.**

- **Assistant A has serious code and logic problems.** It uses `useEffect` without importing it, and its `SmartTextExpand` class calls hooks in the class body, which is invalid React. It also uses unsupported or inappropriate props, including `visibility` on `ActivityIndicator` and `accessibleLabel` on `View`. Its overflow logic relies on character counts or explicit newlines rather than actual rendered lines. The default is two lines, the “See more” control is separate from the text, and the control can appear even when the text does not overflow.
- **Assistant B is much closer to the requested component and is substantially simpler.** However, its `onTextLayout` is attached to a `<Text>` with `numberOfLines={1}` while collapsed. That layout is already limited to one line, so `lines.length > 1` generally cannot detect the overflow. Also, appending the link after the full text does not ensure it remains visible: the native one-line truncation may cut it off. It therefore does not reliably implement its central behavior.

Neither answer adequately handles the key issue: measuring the actual layout and ensuring the inline link fits at the end of the displayed line. Assistant A also includes several unrelated features and an invalid second component, making its answer less useful overall. Assistant B is more relevant and concise, but its overflow detection must be fixed before it can be used.

My final verdict is that Assistant B is significantly better: [[B>>A]]

